## Solution

We evaluate $\displaystyle I=\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{\sin^{2}t}{t^{2}}\,dt$ using Laplace transforms.

### Key property (division by $t$)

If $F(s)=\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\}(s)=\int_{0}^{\infty}f(t)e^{-st}\,dt$, then

$$\mathcal{L}\!\left\{\frac{f(t)}{t}\right\}(s)=\int_{s}^{\infty}F(u)\,du,$$

and in particular, when the integrals converge,

$$\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{f(t)}{t}\,dt=\int_{0}^{\infty}F(s)\,ds. \tag{$\star$}$$

### Step 1. Laplace transform of $\sin^{2}t$

Using $\sin^{2}t=\tfrac{1-\cos 2t}{2}$,

$$F(s)=\mathcal{L}\{\sin^{2}t\}(s)=\frac12\!\left(\frac1s-\frac{s}{s^{2}+4}\right)=\frac{2}{s(s^{2}+4)}. \tag{1}$$

### Step 2. First application of $(\star)$: transform of $\sin^{2}t/t$

Let $g(t)=\dfrac{\sin^{2}t}{t}$. By the division-by-$t$ property and (1),

$$G(s)=\mathcal{L}\{g(t)\}(s)=\int_{s}^{\infty}\frac{2}{u(u^{2}+4)}\,du.$$

Partial fractions give $\dfrac{2}{u(u^{2}+4)}=\dfrac12\!\left(\dfrac1u-\dfrac{u}{u^{2}+4}\right)$, hence

$$G(s)=\frac12\!\left[\ln u-\tfrac12\ln(u^{2}+4)\right]_{s}^{\infty}
=\frac12\!\left[\ln\frac{u}{\sqrt{u^{2}+4}}\right]_{s}^{\infty}
=\frac12\!\left(0-\ln\frac{s}{\sqrt{s^{2}+4}}\right)
=\frac14\ln\frac{s^{2}+4}{s^{2}}. \tag{2}$$

(The boundary term at $\infty$ vanishes since $u/\sqrt{u^{2}+4}\to 1$.)

### Step 3. Second application of $(\star)$: the desired integral

Since $\dfrac{\sin^{2}t}{t^{2}}=\dfrac{g(t)}{t}$, applying $(\star)$ to $g$ and using (2),

$$I=\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{g(t)}{t}\,dt=\int_{0}^{\infty}G(s)\,ds
=\frac14\int_{0}^{\infty}\ln\!\left(1+\frac{4}{s^{2}}\right)ds. \tag{3}$$

### Step 4. Evaluating the remaining integral

Define $\displaystyle J(a)=\int_{0}^{\infty}\ln\!\left(1+\frac{a^{2}}{s^{2}}\right)ds$ for $a\ge 0$.

**Convergence.** Near $s=0$: $\ln(1+a^{2}/s^{2})\sim 2\ln(a/s)$, integrable since $\int_{0}^{1}|\ln s|\,ds<\infty$. Near $s=\infty$: $\ln(1+a^{2}/s^{2})\sim a^{2}/s^{2}$, integrable. So $J(a)$ is well defined.

**Differentiation under the integral.** For $a>0$,

$$J'(a)=\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{2a/s^{2}}{1+a^{2}/s^{2}}\,ds
=\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{2a}{s^{2}+a^{2}}\,ds
=2a\cdot\frac{\pi}{2a}=\pi.$$

(The derivative $\frac{2a}{s^{2}+a^{2}}$ is integrable and dominates the difference quotients on compact $a$-intervals, justifying the interchange by dominated convergence.)

Since $J(0)=0$ and $J'(a)=\pi$ for all $a>0$,

$$J(a)=\pi a. \tag{4}$$

### Step 5. Conclusion

Setting $a=2$ in (4): $\displaystyle\int_{0}^{\infty}\ln\!\left(1+\frac{4}{s^{2}}\right)ds=J(2)=2\pi$.

Substituting into (3):

$$I=\frac14\cdot 2\pi=\frac{\pi}{2}.$$

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{\sin^{2}t}{t^{2}}\,dt=\frac{\pi}{2}.}$$

